--- body ---
## Context

The registry `data/jetp/sources.csv` has only one `publisher` column, and the
collector reasons by host. Yet the host is often neither the author nor the
publisher: `drive.usercontent.google.com` (7 sources) and
`data.commoncrawl.org` host documents of others; the bucket
`cff-prod.s3.amazonaws.com` serves the C40 Cities Finance Facility;
`afd.dgmarket.com` is AFD's procurement provider. The author asks that the
ontology clearly distinguish **responsible party** (who answers for the content),
**publisher** (who publishes) and **distributor** (who serves the bytes):
« on reste dans les considérants de gestion documentaire de base » [we stay
within basic document-management considerations].

Periodic publications (annual and quarterly reports, JETP secretariat progress
reports) form a **series**: one publisher, one pattern, one periodicity; each
**issue** is a document frozen once published, a corrigendum makes it a new
version.

## Actions

1. The three roles enter the source registry schema and the ontology
   (`config/jetp-ledger.sql`, served views), in normal form: one table per
   entity, joins at read time.
2. Series and their issues enter the ontology, with the expected periodicity.
3. The 301 existing sources receive their roles; cases where distributor and
   publisher differ are checked by hand.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every active source has a responsible party, a publisher and a distributor; the distributor is deduced from the host, the other two are filled in
- [ ] Sources served by a third party (Google Drive, Common Crawl, S3, dgMarket) have a distributor distinct from their publisher, backed by a test
- [ ] At least one real series (published issues, periodicity) is recorded and re-read
- [ ] The public site shows publisher and distributor when they differ
- [ ] `make check-fast` and `make lint` green
